# THE BUILTIN SETS, in the same grammar a user writes (V22).
#
# Every member is written U+XXXX, never as the character itself, so this
# file is pure ASCII and needs no grant from `.ctrm` to pass the tool's own
# check (`.:V13`). The cost is that a reader cannot see the characters, so
# each line names them.
#
# These are FUNCTION presets, sized from R2 and R4: a typical file is
# `ascii` plus ONE of these after the map runs (V23). The coarse blocks at
# the bottom are the exception and carry their own warning.
#
# `ascii` is NOT here. It is the one set that must survive
# `--no-builtin-sets`, so it lives in code (`src/rules:V21`).
#
# A `#: <name> <description>` line is a comment to the set grammar and
# DATA to `ctrm sets` (`src/cli/explain:V127`): the curated presets, in
# the order they are listed, each with the one line that says what it is
# for. A preset without one is not curated and lists after them.
#: ascii printable ASCII, tab and newline: the default everywhere

# The symbols a caveman-compressed document is made of. The first eleven are
# FORMAT.md's own list; the rest are what the corpus actually contained, and
# R3 measured that stopping at FORMAT.md's list misses 32% of the files.
#
# RIGHTWARDS ARROW, THEREFORE, FOR ALL, THERE EXISTS, UP TACK, NOT EQUAL TO,
# ELEMENT OF, NOT AN ELEMENT OF, LESS-THAN OR EQUAL TO, GREATER-THAN OR
# EQUAL TO, SECTION SIGN, RIGHTWARDS DOUBLE ARROW, BECAUSE, MIDDLE DOT,
# MULTIPLICATION SIGN, LOGICAL OR, LOGICAL AND, NOT SIGN, LEFTWARDS ARROW,
# LEFT RIGHT ARROW, LEFT RIGHT DOUBLE ARROW, ALMOST EQUAL TO, EMPTY SET,
# SUBSET OF OR EQUAL TO, UNION, INTERSECTION, UPWARDS ARROW, DOWNWARDS ARROW,
# then four R19 found in 4-6 of 13 Rust repos that no preset held: IDENTICAL
# TO, N-ARY SUMMATION as the Greek letter, LONG LEFT RIGHT DOUBLE ARROW,
# SUPERSET OF OR EQUAL TO.
#: caveman the logic and arrow symbols compressed notation uses
caveman U+2192 U+2234 U+2200 U+2203 U+22A5 U+2260 U+2208 U+2209 U+2264 U+2265 U+00A7 U+21D2 U+2235 U+00B7 U+00D7 U+2228 U+2227 U+00AC U+2190 U+2194 U+21D4 U+2248 U+2205 U+2286 U+222A U+2229 U+2191 U+2193 U+2261 U+03A3 U+27FA U+2287

# Box Drawing, then Geometric Shapes: the two blocks a drawn diagram uses.
# Granted whole rather than character by character -- a diagram reaches for
# whichever corner and shade it needs, and a set that held only the ones
# seen so far would fail on the next drawing.
#: box box drawing and geometric shapes
box U+2500-U+257F U+25A0-U+25FF

# The mathematics that appears in prose rather than in a formula.
#
# MULTIPLICATION SIGN, DIVISION SIGN, PLUS-MINUS SIGN, DEGREE SIGN,
# SUPERSCRIPT TWO, SUPERSCRIPT THREE, LESS-THAN OR EQUAL TO, GREATER-THAN OR
# EQUAL TO, NOT EQUAL TO, ALMOST EQUAL TO, INFINITY, MICRO SIGN, SQUARE
# ROOT, N-ARY SUMMATION, GREEK SMALL LETTER PI
#: math the mathematics that turns up in prose
math U+00D7 U+00F7 U+00B1 U+00B0 U+00B2 U+00B3 U+2264 U+2265 U+2260 U+2248 U+221E U+00B5 U+221A U+2211 U+03C0

# COPYRIGHT SIGN, REGISTERED SIGN, TRADE MARK SIGN. R5 found these across
# the fleet and nothing maps them: a notice that says `(c)` is a different
# notice.
#: legal copyright, registered, trade mark
legal U+00A9 U+00AE U+2122

# Real typography, which is a GRANT and not a default (V26).
#
# The builtin map targets every one of these, and R4 measured that the map
# alone settles 45.1% of non-ASCII files. Granting this set is how a file
# says the map should leave them alone instead (`src/fix:V6`).
#
# EM DASH, EN DASH, LEFT and RIGHT SINGLE QUOTATION MARK, LEFT and RIGHT
# DOUBLE QUOTATION MARK, HORIZONTAL ELLIPSIS, LEFT-POINTING and
# RIGHT-POINTING DOUBLE ANGLE QUOTATION MARK, NO-BREAK SPACE, MINUS SIGN,
# then SINGLE and DOUBLE LOW-9 and HIGH-REVERSED-9 QUOTATION MARK: the
# map targets those too (B73), so a granted Polish quotation keeps both
# of its quotes rather than losing the opening one alone.
#: typography real dashes, curly and low-9 quotes, ellipsis, no-break space
typography U+2014 U+2013 U+2018 U+2019 U+201C U+201D U+2026 U+00AB U+00BB U+00A0 U+2212 U+201A U+201B U+201E U+201F

# Emoji as SINGLE code points only (V31). No variation selector, no skin
# tone modifier, no zero-width joiner and no keycap: a sequence therefore
# always contains a code point this set withholds, which is what makes the
# builtin sequence map the thing that compresses it rather than a grant
# quietly admitting both spellings.
#
# Miscellaneous Symbols and Pictographs through Symbols and Pictographs
# Extended-A, then the older Miscellaneous Symbols and Dingbats blocks --
# with the five EMOJI MODIFIER FITZPATRICK code points cut OUT of the
# first span. They sit inside it, and V3 grants no way to take a character
# back once a range has given it, so the gap has to be in how the range is
# written. `src/charset/SPEC.md` I states the block unbroken; V31 is the
# narrower rule and wins.
#: emoji emoji as single code points; a sequence compresses to one
emoji U+1F300-U+1F3FA U+1F400-U+1FAFF U+2600-U+27BF

# The status marks, in BOTH spellings, each labelled with the fidelity it
# belongs to (V41). A rule resolving `marks` at `text` grants the first
# three and at `emoji` the last three, so a file never quietly admits both
# spellings of one mark and the map compresses the other into the granted
# one (`src/fix:V28`).
#
# WARNING SIGN carries no label: it is the same code point either way, and
# labelling it twice would say there were two.
#
# CHECK MARK, BALLOT X, RIGHTWARDS ARROW @ text; WHITE HEAVY CHECK MARK,
# CROSS MARK, BLACK RIGHTWARDS ARROW @ emoji.
#: marks check, cross, warning, arrow -- in both spellings
marks U+26A0 text:U+2713 text:U+2717 text:U+2192 emoji:U+2705 emoji:U+274C emoji:U+27A1

# CARRIAGE RETURN, alone. Separate from `ascii` because a file granting it
# is making a decision about LINE ENDINGS, not about characters, and that
# decision deserves to be visible in the rule that makes it.
#: cr carriage return, alone
cr U+000D

# COARSE BLOCKS. V23 recommends these ONLY for multi-language data such as
# locale files -- not for code and not for docs. A block grants hundreds of
# characters, so it answers "this file is in that script" rather than "this
# file needs these characters", and the second question is the one a token
# budget is asking. For a language, prefer the letter preset named by its
# locale code, which is generated from CLDR (`src/charset/locale:V30`).
#
# Latin-1 Supplement; Latin Extended-A; Cyrillic; Greek and Coptic; Arabic.
#: latin1 coarse block, Latin-1 Supplement: multi-language data only
latin1 U+0080-U+00FF
#: latin-ext coarse block, Latin Extended-A: multi-language data only
latin-ext U+0100-U+017F
#: cyrillic coarse block, Cyrillic: multi-language data only
cyrillic U+0400-U+04FF
#: greek coarse block, Greek and Coptic: multi-language data only
greek U+0370-U+03FF
#: arabic coarse block, Arabic: multi-language data only
arabic U+0600-U+06FF

# SCRIPTS WHOSE SPELLING NEEDS A JOINER (`src/lint/hazard:V57`). Persian writes
# ZERO WIDTH NON-JOINER inside words; Devanagari uses it and ZERO WIDTH
# JOINER to choose conjunct forms. Both are hazards everywhere else, and
# naming one of these presets is the explicit grant that says this file is
# in that script. Nothing else is excused: `persian` leaves out U+061C,
# ARABIC LETTER MARK, because a bidi control stays a hazard in any script.
#: persian Persian script, with the zero width non-joiner it spells with
persian U+0600-U+061B U+061D-U+06FF U+200C
#: hindi Devanagari script, with the joiners it spells with
hindi U+0900-U+097F U+200C-U+200D

# Every code point there is, which is how a file opts OUT of being checked
# while staying in the configuration rather than disappearing from it: a
# path granted `any` still has a rule, an origin, and a line `explain` can
# print. The gap is the surrogate range, which holds no character.
#: any everything, for a file you do not own -- hazards still fire
any U+0000-U+D7FF U+E000-U+10FFFF

# The generated hazard sets live in `hazard.ctrm-sets`, which has no
# hand-written lines; their descriptions are kept here, with the rest.
#: hazard invisible and reordering characters: never granted, always fail
#: hazard-bidi bidi controls, which reorder what a reader sees
#: hazard-tag tag characters: invisible ASCII a model still reads
#: hazard-bom a byte order mark anywhere but byte 0
#: hazard-control C0 and C1 controls other than tab, newline and carriage return
#: hazard-invisible zero width and other invisible characters
